00:00:08.770 --> 00:00:11.170
CARTER MORGAN: Let's talk
about Kf, a new tool that

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.960
lets you quickly modernize
your Cloud Foundry workloads

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.850
and use the same Cloud Foundry
concepts you know and love,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.220
but on Kubernetes.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:20.380
If you're a platform
owner using Cloud Foundry,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.110
you're not going to
want to miss this video.

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:25.810
Cloud Foundry's easy-to-use
developer workflow

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:29.140
allowed application teams to
almost magically deploy code

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.229
to an application infrastructure
they knew nothing about,

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.800
and in just a couple of minutes
using a few terminal commands.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:39.790
But over time, Cloud Foundry as
a platform has become legacy.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.050
Applications evolved.

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:42.850
Microservices exploded.

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.010
Customers moved to the
cloud using Kubernetes

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:47.110
as their container orchestrator.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:49.840
But many of these customers
wanted a familiar, intuitive

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.150
developer abstraction
on top of Kubernetes.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.710
So how could you
move to Kubernetes

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.010
and maintain that same
developer experience?

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.320
That's where Kf comes into play.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.270
Kf provides a smooth
stepping stone

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:04.090
from what you had
before to what's next.

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:05.920
It allows you to
modernize workloads

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.990
by running them
on GKE and Anthos,

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:10.930
but what makes Kf special is
that it minimizes developer

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.170
downtime by allowing developers
to retain the same Cloud

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.718
Foundry workflow
they already use.

00:01:16.718 --> 00:01:18.760
So just like in Cloud
Foundry, when you typically

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.070
type the cf push command to
build and deploy your code,

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:24.490
you can do the same with
Kf by saying kf push.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.040
So same workflow,
but now instead

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.750
of operating three to
four updates behind

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:31.810
because of complex tooling,
you can use up-to-date tooling

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:32.950
and dependencies.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.140
You get the advantages of
integrating new updates

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:35.860
quickly.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.110
And once you're on the cloud,
it becomes a lot simpler

00:01:38.110 --> 00:01:41.230
to try different managed
or unmanaged products.

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:44.368
You see, at its core, Kf
is an abstraction layer

00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:45.910
that makes it easier
for applications

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.180
to run on Kubernetes,
GKE, or even Anthos.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:51.550
Kf becomes your
bridge to the cloud,

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.820
providing an easy process
for modernizing workloads.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.862
Not only that, once
you're using Google Cloud,

00:01:56.862 --> 00:01:59.320
billing becomes straightforward
and costs get significantly

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.190
reduced because Google Cloud
understands modern workloads.

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:05.200
Instead of paying
commercial licensing fees,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.050
your organization can
just now run Kubernetes.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.630
Instead of maintaining a Pivotal
Cloud Foundry environment

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:12.683
and a cloud
architecture, with Kf,

00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:14.350
you can save costs
by running everything

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.250
on a single platform.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.720
But don't take my word for it.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.960
Be sure to watch CoreLogic's
Cloud Solution Architect Jeff

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.260
Henry's Google Next talk,
which details their transition

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.570
to the cloud using Kf.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.630
In it, you'll see how CoreLogic
replatformed 10,000-plus Cloud

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.410
Foundry applications using Kf.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.910
You'll learn how this tooling
minimized developer disruption

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:39.240
by retaining the Cloud
Foundry experience, while also

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.150
standardizing platform
automation on Kubernetes.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.430
And you'll review the
assessment and migration

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.190
tools CoreLogic used to
automate and accelerate

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.830
their migration to Kubernetes.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.830
To wrap up, I want to point
out three use cases that

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.620
make Kf an excellent
tool for platform admins

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.660
to quickly modernize
their infrastructure

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.140
using Kubernetes.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:03.200
One, to help you develop
a migration plan,

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.420
you can use the kf
command-line tool

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.700
to assess your current
Cloud Foundry environment

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:11.630
and create an inventory of all
the Cloud Foundry resources.

00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:15.170
Two, you can also use Kf
as a drop-in replacement

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.080
for the Cloud Foundry CLI
to keep the same developer

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.340
workflow as before.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:23.700
Just swap cf push for kf push
and you should be good to go.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.390
And finally, at
runtime, Kf can be

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.330
used to provide an abstraction
layer over Cloud Foundry

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.040
so that Cloud Foundry
applications can

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.200
run on Kubernetes unchanged.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.700
So this is my call to action
for any Cloud Foundry customer.

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.520
If you're running an open
source or any commercial version

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.770
of Cloud Foundry, try Kf.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.540
It's a bridge to
getting your application

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.180
running on modern tooling
like Kubernetes and GKE,

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:50.760
which is the number one managed
Kubernetes distribution.

